  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA frontal boundary and weak H5 trof will cross the area today,\nproviding a forcing mechanism for ascent. Scattered showers this\nmorning will likely become more widespread this afternoon with\npartly sunny skies mixed in between. Forecast soundings from\nlatest CAMs show some modest daytime instability with MUCAPE and\nMLCAPE up to around 500 J/KG and up to 35 kts of 0-6km bulk\nshear. This may allow for a few gusty thunderstorms to develop\nwith a few potentially becoming close to severe thresholds. High\ntemperatures will be into the upper 70s to lower 80s south of\nthe mountains with lower to middle 70s towards the Canadian\nBorder.\n\nShowers and storms will wane tonight with low temperatures\nfalling to around 50 across the interior valleys with middle to\nupper 50s elsewhere. Valley fog is possible.\n\nHigh pressure will return for Friday into Friday night, allowing\nfor dry conditions with mainly sunny skies and clear conditions\nat night. High temperatures will be into the 70s to lower 80s\nfrom north to south with low temperatures into the 40s across\ninterior valleys with 50s elsewhere. Valley fog is once again\npossible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA sharp trough axis moves east of the area Saturday with the \nincreased northwesterly flow dropping 850 mb temps down to +9 to\n10C. Normally this would only be good for temperatures in the \nlow to mid 70s (which is likely north of the mountains), but \nnorthwesterly surface flow tends to give us boost from \ndownsloping winds south of the mountains so we could see closer \nto the upper 70s and low 80s. Ridging aloft and surface high \npressure will keep it mostly dry. This persists through the \nfirst half of Sunday with dry weather and similar temperatures. \nSunday night is when the weather looks to take a turn with low \npressure approaching the region. Global models continue to agree\non this, so confidence is increasing that we see some rain \nSunday night into Monday. However, the point of contention is \nthe track and therefore the spread in how much rainfall we may \nsee is high with the 25th-75th percentiles ranging from nada to \n0.75\". The ECMWF Ensemble is currently on the high end with the \ntrack of the low going overhead and then deepening as it moves \noff the coast. The GEFS and GEPS take the track more south of \nthe area with the GEFS having the weaker low and the the GEPS \nending up in the middle with some strengthening over the gulf of\nMaine. Current NBM probabilities of around a half inch of rain \nare low (30-40%). \n  \nThe pattern remains unsettled into midweek as broad troughing \ndevelops at 500 mb once again. Southwesterly flow will likely \nbegin a warming trend with the track of surface high pressure \ndetermining how warm we get, and ultimately the chances for \nprecipitation as well.",
